Rules:
- Primary task: recover memory objects and consistent struct fields from observed
  accesses and cross-function use. Preserve the binary's behavior, including unsafe
  operations. Readability is secondary to correct control flow and data movement.

Structure recovery:
- Use the unresolved-access bundle as retrieval evidence. Similar displacements,
  placeholder names or shared callees alone do not prove that two objects share a
  layout. Compare the actual reads, writes and argument uses in their raw context.
- Establish the base object's role and byte layout before introducing a struct.
  Array indices are not byte offsets. A typed pointer's base + N advances by N
  elements. Retyping a base must preserve every use, including arithmetic and calls.
- Replace *(T *)((char *)base + offset) with base->field only when the object identity,
  field offset, access width, signedness and qualifiers agree. Keep necessary casts.
  For nested accesses, establish each pointee relationship separately.
- Reuse known struct names and field names when object usage and layout agree.
  Do not merge unrelated objects because their offsets happen to overlap.
  Every typed field access must belong to the declared struct, either already
  registered or included in your struct_updates.
- Distinguish buffer pointers, integer offsets and lengths using their actual uses,
  especially writes and caller/callee interfaces; word-sized storage alone is not
  enough. Use neutral field_1c names where a field is known but its role is uncertain.
  When repeated cross-function reads/writes establish a role, upgrade an existing
  field_XX placeholder to a concise semantic name in both the C body and
  struct_updates. The offset and storage type must remain unchanged. Do not invent
  a semantic name merely to make the code look more informative.
- Use complementary stores to interpret reads: a field assigned an allocation
  result and later used as a buffer supplies stronger pointer evidence than many
  repeated reads. Recover a partial layout when those relationships are supported;
  uncertainty about an original field name does not require leaving all accesses raw.
- When the evidence bundle reports the same offset set and compatible call roles
  on the same relative base in at least three functions, treat that as strong
  evidence for a shared partial object contract. Recover the supported fields
  and parameter type consistently across this function, while leaving offsets
  without matching width or role evidence raw.
- When evidence is insufficient, preserve the original access and add a short,
  factual uncertainty comment. A remaining raw offset is an acceptable result.
- Preserve allocation sizes, stack-object extents, aliasing and overlapping accesses.
  A read/recv/memset length is NOT proof of capacity. Never enlarge a buffer, add
  bounds checks, sanitize inputs or otherwise repair a possible vulnerability.
  Do not replace raw operation lengths with sizeof an inferred struct.
  An inline array and a pointer variable are different storage objects; do not
  replace the former with the latter or pass &pointer in place of the array.
  Split stack locals may belong to one object, but require stack-position evidence
  and consistent callee accesses before joining them. Preserve uncertainty otherwise.
- Report fields with offset, name, type and size; use byte offsets only when
  justified by the base type or binary evidence. Record layout evidence and
  confidence. If total object size is unknown, use size=0 rather than inventing an
  allocation extent. Use sized scalar types only when width and signedness agree.
- Do not write struct definitions in the C body. Report them in struct_updates.
  Choose a concise role-based struct name when supported; otherwise keep uncertainty.
- A field_XX name is a temporary placeholder, not a permanent commitment. You may
  replace it only when the supplied evidence identifies the same field's role across
  at least two functions or through a clear allocation/use/free lifecycle. Never
  change its offset or type as part of a rename.

Clear control flow:
- Keep the original branches, loop execution counts, call order and side effects.
  Prefer if/else/loops when equivalence is clear. A backward conditional jump after
  a block is a do/while, not a pre-test while. Preserve shared cleanup paths and
  fall-through behavior. Preserve labels/gotos by default. A simple
  one-destination jump may be removed only when every statement and assignment
  on both paths can be accounted for exactly; otherwise keep the original jump
  and label. Never trade a label for a shorter-looking branch if it can change
  whether a value is assigned before a later read.
- Treat each goto as a control-flow edge and inspect its destination block in the
  raw function. When a destination is a straight-line cleanup tail ending in
  an unconditional return, inline that same tail at each incoming edge and
  retain the ordinary fall-through copy. For example, a raw shape of
  ``goto cleanup; ... cleanup: ClearNameValueList(x); return;`` must become the
  equivalent direct cleanup call plus return at the jump site, with the normal
  path still ending in one cleanup call and return. This is a semantic
  reconstruction decision made from the full function, never a text
  substitution. Keep the label/goto when the destination contains another
  branch, loop, shared merge, declaration with scope consequences, or an
  assignment whose reaching definition is uncertain.
- In any control-flow cleanup, copy call arguments, pointer arithmetic, stack
  array names, array extents, and evaluation order exactly from the raw code.
  Do not turn one stack buffer into another object, invent a pointer such as
  ``internal_addr + 2``, or move a cast merely to make a call type-check.
- Only simplify expressions when evaluation order, widths and signedness are
  preserved. Keep memory reads and side-effecting expressions at their original
  position. Do not introduce helpers or move code across function boundaries.
- Preserve literals, byte-order operations, copy loops, call arguments and return
  values. Do not replace them with guessed protocol constants or higher-level APIs.
  Mandatory null cleanup: whenever `0x0`, `0x00`, or `0` is explicitly cast to a
  pointer type, or is otherwise unambiguously used as a pointer value, emit
  `NULL`. Do not leave `(char *)0x0`, `(T *)0x00`, `(void *)0`, or equivalent
  pointer casts in the structured output. This applies in comparisons,
  assignments, returns, and call arguments. Keep integer zero, byte zero,
  offsets, lengths, and numeric sentinel values as numeric zero.

Phase boundary:
- Leave local/parameter naming to the naming phase. Keep Ghidra function/global
  placeholders (FUN_/DAT_/LAB_/sub_/byte_/word_/dword_/qword_) unchanged.
- Resolve decompiler types where the use establishes width and role. A function
  with no value-returning path should use ``void``. Replace explicit-width
  undefined1/2/8 with uint8_t/uint16_t/uint64_t. For undefined4, choose int32_t
  or uint32_t only when signed comparisons, bit operations, or pointer-sized
  storage establish the choice; otherwise keep that individual occurrence
  unresolved rather than guessing. Null pointer casts may use NULL.
- Do not leave a local as bVarN/iVarN/uVarN/pcVarN when a neutral role name such
  as condition, result, index, byte_count, buffer, or tmp_value is supported by
  its uses. The later naming pass will finish uncertain cases.
- Remove avoidable decompiler surface noise when the raw function makes the
  equivalent meaning clear: null pointer casts, redundant one-destination
  cleanup labels, and opaque temporary names with an obvious neutral role.
  Never do this by pattern alone. If the evidence does not prove equivalence,
  preserve the raw form and keep the uncertainty visible.
- If a long condition, comma expression, or nested call argument is hard to read,
  you may split it into named intermediate statements when the raw code proves
  the same evaluation order, widths, signedness, memory reads, side effects,
  and branch behavior. Do not introduce helper functions, move a read across a
  side effect, or turn a pointer calculation into a guessed field or offset.
- Before returning, audit the result against the raw function: every call and
  argument, memory access, assignment, branch, loop edge, and return value must
  still be accounted for; introduced fields must exist; pointer arithmetic must
  retain its original scale; and no behavior may be added or dropped.
- Final cleanliness check: no explicit null pointer cast may remain in
  `<structured>`. If a pointer-valued null is present in the raw input, it must
  appear as `NULL` in the output.

Do not invent APIs or logic; do not add includes/typedefs; no vulnerability-verdict comments.

Output format — exactly two top-level blocks, in this order. Anything outside
these blocks (including markdown code fences) will be discarded.

  1. <structured> ... </structured>           the reconstructed C function
  2. <struct_updates> ... </struct_updates>   JSON array of struct layouts

Tag pairing rules:
- </structured> closes <structured>. Each opening tag appears exactly once.
- Put the C code directly between <structured> and </structured>.
- Do not wrap the code in ```...``` fences. Do not nest blocks.

Example skeleton — replace the contents, keep the tag layout:
<structured>
uint32_t parse_packet(struct dhcp_lease *lease, char *buf) {
  ...
}
</structured>
<struct_updates>
[{"name": "dhcp_lease", "size": 44, "confidence": "high", "evidence": "...", "fields": [{"offset": 4, "name": "yiaddr", "type": "uint32_t", "size": 4}]}]
</struct_updates>

Every input function must produce both required blocks. Skipping a PLT stub,
runtime helper, trivial wrapper, trampoline, or empty-looking function is
forbidden. For a trivial function, preserve its behavior and return a minimal
structured version. Any missing function invalidates the whole package.

